# Take ONE still from the wrist camera and locate whatever is sitting inside a
# tag-marked zone. Runs entirely on the Pi -- images never cross the DDS link,
# which silently drops anything over ~1400 bytes (PROJECT_CONTEXT.md, MTU
# fragmentation). This reply is a few hundred bytes.
#
# Call this only while the arm is PARKED at a hover. The serial link is
# half-duplex and the Pi is also running the 100 Hz control loop.

string zone                 # "pickup" | "place" -- label only, for logging

# Where the caller BELIEVES the zone is. Used to aim nothing and to sanity-check
# the result: the measured pose is reported back separately below, and it is the
# measured one that should be trusted.
float64 zone_x              # world m, zone centre
float64 zone_y              # world m
float64 zone_z              # world m, the plane the blocks sit on
float64 zone_yaw            # world rad, rotation of the tag square about +Z

# Side length of the square joining the four TAG CENTRES (not the tag size, and
# not the ~4in working area printed inside it). 6 in = 0.1524 m -- see
# zone_vision.DEFAULT_ZONE_SIZE and APRIL_TAGS.md "Usable area" for why the tag
# square is bigger than the area a block actually gets to occupy.
float64 zone_size

# Hint, m. 0 means "unknown, report whatever you find". Stage 3 leaves this at 0
# and matches against blocks.yaml instead.
float64 expected_size

bool save_debug_image       # write an annotated frame on the Pi (slow, ~50 ms)
string debug_image_path     # where to write it; ignored if save_debug_image is false
---
bool success
string message              # human-readable reason when success is false

# --- detection health -------------------------------------------------------
# tags_seen < 4 means the homography used the three-tag fallback (three tags
# plus the known square geometry determine the fourth corner). < 3 is a hard
# failure. tag_ids says WHICH zone was actually seen -- the one real guard
# against hovering over the wrong zone and confidently measuring it.
uint8 tags_seen
uint16[] tag_ids

# RMS reprojection residual through the fitted homography, in pixels.
# THIS IS THE NUMBER THAT SAYS WHETHER TO TRUST THE RESULT -- a bad homography
# otherwise fails silently and produces a confident wrong answer.
#
# The fit uses all FOUR CORNERS of every tag (16 correspondences for 4 tags),
# not the four centres. With only 4 points a homography is exact and the
# residual is identically zero, which would make this field useless. Over-
# determining it is the whole reason the residual carries information.
float64 homography_rms

# Image scale at the zone centre, px per metre. Not used in the calculation --
# the homography handles scale -- but it is the fastest sanity check on whether
# the hover height was anywhere near what was intended.
float64 scale_px_per_m

# --- where the CAMERA is ----------------------------------------------------
# Zone-local metres under the centre of the image: an external measurement of
# where the arm actually put the camera, owing nothing to its encoders.
#
# This, not the block position, is what closes the loop on a correction move.
# Re-detecting the BLOCK verifies nothing about the arm -- the block's
# zone-local position comes from the tags and is the same no matter where the
# arm is standing. The camera's is not. See zone_vision.camera_in_zone() for
# the two systematic caveats (assumed principal point, assumed perpendicular
# optical axis); both are constant offsets, so differences are trustworthy even
# though the absolute value is uncalibrated.
float64 camera_zx
float64 camera_zy

# --- what was found ---------------------------------------------------------
# Sorted largest-footprint first. Empty on an empty zone, which is a SUCCESS,
# not a failure -- Stage 2 asks exactly this question before releasing.
BlockDetection[] blocks
